1	en	154	0x9a230	39459	13	13792	14767	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0160_000_0	17	"deniable" covert paramilitary operations.\n
1	en	154	0x9a230	39459	14	14837	15896	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0160_000_0	17	One of those units trained the Bolivian Army in\ncounter-guerilla tactics....\n
1	en	154	0x9a230	39459	15	15924	16537	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0160_000_0	17	...And then had them shoot El Che.\n
1	en	154	0x9a230	39459	16	16551	17052	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0160_000_0	17	So the story goes.\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	0	501	1657	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	Around here, they say "La CIA", instead of "C-I-A,"\nhuh?\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	1	2117	3218	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	Nothing strange about it. That's how it's\npronounced when you read it in Spanish.\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	2	3232	4472	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	It has the feminine noun ending "a," so they use\nthe article "la."\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	3	4486	5572	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	Apparently some people have even taken to using\nthe term UCLA.\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	4	5586	6241	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	That's a new one to me. What's it mean?\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	5	6241	7328	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	It stands for "Unilaterally Controlled Latino Assets."\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	6	7342	7774	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	...Meaning their local agents?\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	7	7787	8791	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	Yeah, that's the idea. Washington uses them like\npawns,\n
1	en	154	0x9a7f0	39551	8	8791	9571	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0170_000_0	9	and nobody knows who they really are or what\nthey're doing.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	0				v_bri_kaz018	17	Can you believe the CIA and the KGB were actually\nin cahoots?\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	1				v_bri_kaz018	17	Never thought I'd see that at the station chief\nlevel...\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	2				v_bri_kaz018	17	But I've heard stories of operatives in the field\nbeing on friendly terms.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	3				v_bri_kaz018	17	Seriously?\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	4				v_bri_kaz018	17	Most espionage takes place in political hotspots, as\nyou'd expect.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	5				v_bri_kaz018	17	But there aren't that many of those kinds of\nplaces. Especially in a small country.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	6				v_bri_kaz018	17	Hang around long enough, and you're bound to run\ninto fellow spies, like it or not.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	7				v_bri_kaz018	17	You start saying hello, and soon enough you're\neating dinner together...\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	8				v_bri_kaz018	17	Strange bedfellows, huh?\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	9				v_bri_kaz018	17	I dunno about that.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	10				v_bri_kaz018	17	At any rate, this kind of fiasco is what happens\nwhen spies get too familiar.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	11				v_bri_kaz018	17	Hard to believe,\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	12				v_bri_kaz018	17	but some people will betray anyone and anything if\nit suits their interests.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	13				v_bri_kaz018	17	The strength of HUMINT is in its appeal to human\nemotions,\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	14				v_bri_kaz018	17	but its weakness is in its susceptibility to those\nsame emotions.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	15				v_bri_kaz018	17	That's why the tide in the intelligence world is\nshifting toward SIGINT.\n
1	en	154	0x9ab70	39607	16				v_bri_kaz018	17	A network of cold, digital data, where feelings are\nirrelevant.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	0				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Snake, you got a sec?\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	1				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	What's up?\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	2				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Prime Minister Gairy of Grenada is asking the UN\nto set up an agency\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	3				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	to study the "UFO problem."\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	4				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Grenada... That island in the Caribbean?\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	5				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	He gave the press a bunch of photos - says they\nprove the existence of UFOs.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	6				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	One of them caught my eye.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	7				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	That's the...\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	8				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Yep... The weapon Huey called the Chrysalis.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	9				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	...Kaz, this is the photo I gave to Chico.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	10				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	He said something about selling it off as a photo\nof a UFO...\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	11				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Looks like it found its way into the pages of a\nmagazine.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	12				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	But... why would the prime minister of a country\nbelieve tabloid trash like that?\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	13				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	There's been reports all over the Americas of\nabductions and cattle mutilations, lately.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	14				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	You think they're true?\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	15				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	I think the CIA might be involved.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	16				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	If they are, then Gairy could be...\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	17				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	I know. If he digs too deep, he's gonna get himself\ninto trouble.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	18				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	But Grenada's right next door to Cuba.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	19				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	I guess that would make it tough for the CIA to\nintervene, in a sense.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	20				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Upsetting the domestic balance of power could\ncause it to go Red all of a sudden.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	21				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	True... Well, in any case it's not like we can do\nanything about it.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	22				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Just wanted to let you know.\n
1	en	155	0x9b1c0	39708	23				v_bri_kaz0190_000_0	24	Gotcha. I'll be careful not to let any more photos\nleak.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	0				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	So I hear Amanda mistook you for Che Guevara,\nSnake.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	1				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	That's not too bad, huh?\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	2				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Yeah, right. I'm not even worthy of polishing his\nboots.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	3				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Don't be so modest.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	4				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	From where I'm standing, your men see you as a\ngreat man.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	5				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	As great as "the century's most complete human\nbeing"?\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	6				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	That's Sartre, right? Well, there's hardly been a\nmore iconic figure of his times than Che.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	7				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	He was more than that. He was a true\nrevolutionary, and a great warrior.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	8				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	I'm with you there. Can you believe that when he\nfirst went to Cuba with Fidel,\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	9				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	they only had 12 guys with them?\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	10				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	But they rallied. They brought in new recruits,\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	11				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	won the support of the peasants, expanded their\norganization...\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	12				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	And in the end, they overthrew the Batista regime...\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	13				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	People flocked to them because they were honest.\nThey won because they were strong.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	14				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Those are the qualities that make men great.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	15				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	You know, we're kind of in the same boat they\nwere back then.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	16				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Here we are, a handful of mercenaries\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	17				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	taking on an army backed by the United States.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	18				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Yeah, we've got a long way to go.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	19				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	But we've got to keep on going. It's not just about\nwinning in battle.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	20				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	You need to think about recruiting people and\ngrowing this operation.\n
1	en	155	0x9b990	39833	21				v_bri_kaz0200_000_0	22	Got it.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	0	501	1142	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Hey, Snake, I've been thinking...\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	1	1170	2284	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Maybe Che couldn't find a place for himself outside\nthe battlefield, either.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	2	2298	2535	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	How so?\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	3	2549	3803	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	He led the Cuban Revolution to victory. That's\nquite an accomplishment.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	4	3831	4625	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	He could have called it quits there and nobody\nwould have blamed him.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	5	4653	6102	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	But he left behind a loving family and a cabinet\npost to plunge back into the struggle.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	6	6116	7091	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	First in the Congo, then in Bolivia - where he met\nhis end.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	7	7105	8526	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	You're overthinking things. Che went back to the\nbattlefield because he was needed there.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	8	8582	9376	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Read his "Farewell Letter," then you'll understand.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	9	9390	10351	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Some say he was too idealistic to fit in with a\ngovernment.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	10	10365	11103	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Too many scruples, most likely.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	11	11117	11382	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Maybe so...\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	12	11396	12343	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	But I still can't help thinking the reason you look\nup to Che\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	13	12385	13165	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	is because you see something of yourself in him...\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	14	13235	14071	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	You left your country behind and plunged into\nbattle.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	15	14154	15269	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	You wander from one battlefield to another, going\nwherever you're needed...\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	16	15283	16871	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	Che was a revolutionary as well as a warrior. He\ntook up arms to fight for his beliefs.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	17	16885	18919	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	I don't do what I do for any ideology. I'm a warrior.\nNothing more, nothing less.\n
1	en	156	0x9c120	39954	18	18961	20591	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0200_010_0	19	OK, Boss. You're right. Just wanted to see how we\nstacked up against a real hero.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	0				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Boss, did you ever read Che Guevara's book\n"Guerrilla Warfare"?\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	1				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	I, uh... can't remember if I got to that one or not.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	2				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	You should have, that's why I lent it to you.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	3				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	A lot of the guerrilla tactics it covers apply to\nsneaking missions, too.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	4				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Che was one of the first people to articulate the\ntheory of guerrilla warfare.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	5				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	T.E. Lawrence - "Lawrence of Arabia" - was another\none.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	6				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	And Mao Zedong and his "On Protracted War"...\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	7				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Theory aside, Nicaragua's General Sandino\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	8				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	was one of the first to put guerrilla tactics into\npractice.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	9				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Which explains why the Sandinistas named\nthemselves after him.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	10				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	If you think about it, guerrilla warfare itself has\nbeen around since ancient times.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	11				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	There's only so many ways a small group can\nupset a large army.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	12				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	There was a samurai in Japan who excelled in\nguerrilla warfare.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	13				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	No kidding?\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	14				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Kusunoki Masashige. He was a warrior who lived in\nthe medieval era.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	15				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	He used unconventional tactics to help overthrow\nthe Kamakura Shogunate.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	16				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Like what?\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	17				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Trojan Horse-style maneuvers and decoys to\nconfuse the enemy...\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	18				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	The best one was when the enemy was climbing\nhis castle walls\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	19				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	- he dumped boiling water and human excrement\non them...\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	20				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Sounds great. Let's put it in the MSF playbook.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	21				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	...You're not serious, are you, Boss?\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	22				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Why not? We've got plenty of crap to unload.\n
1	en	156	0x9c850	40069	23				v_bri_kaz0210_000_0	24	Uh.... yeah. I'll think about it.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	0	445	1030	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	What's wrong, Kaz? You sound beat.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	1	1044	2020	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	Yeah, the problems never seem to end around\nhere...\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	2	2034	3190	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	You should take a break. Share a cup of mate with\nthe other guys.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	3	3287	3831	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	It'll give you a chance to connect with them.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	4	3859	4931	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	I wonder if Che and his men ever sat around drank\nmate.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	5	4945	6088	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	I bet they did. Che was famous for his love of the\nstuff.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	6	7523	8930	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	Man, whoever thought of this was a genius. You\ncan put it in a gourd and carry it around,\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	7	8944	10142	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	and there's a special straw with a filter attached\nso you can drink it anytime.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	8	10156	11382	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	That's not all. It's full of essential vitamins and\nminerals, too.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	9	11521	12301	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	Nice to have in a guerrilla war when food is short.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	10	12315	13904	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	Yeah, I wish I had a chance to share some with a\nblonde Parisienne when I was out hiking.\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	11	14043	14461	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	...How do you know about that?\n
1	en	157	0x9d0b0	40203	12	14461	16370	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0230_000_0	13	It takes a thief - or should I say it takes a snake -\nto know one, Snake.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	0	487	1128	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	The year the Cuban Revolution was won,\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	1	1212	2256	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Che visited Japan as a member of an economic\ndelegation.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	2	2368	3037	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	While he was there, he visited Hiroshima.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	3	3037	3301	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Hiroshima...\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	4	3329	4137	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Since he was there to discuss economic issues,\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	5	4207	5057	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Hiroshima wasn't part of the original itinerary.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	6	5126	6631	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Some said the Ministry of Foreign Affairs didn't\nwant to let him go. But he went anyway.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	7	6687	7578	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	He snuck out of his hotel and took an overnight\ntrain.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	8	7829	8930	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Traveling guerrilla-style - sounds like Che, all right.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	9	8958	10309	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	He visited the Peace Memorial Museum and the\nAtomic Bomb Survivors Hospital.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	10	10421	10992	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Apparently it gave him quite a shock.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	11	11020	12204	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	As a doctor, it must have been painful for him to\nsee how the victims suffered.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	12	12232	12733	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	Nukes destroy everything...\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	13	12747	13123	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	He was quoted as saying,\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	14	13263	14475	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	"They put you through this, and still you do\nwhatever America says?"\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	15	14531	15743	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	...Those words really hit me hard. Especially when I\nthink of my mom.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	16	15770	17261	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	He said something else, too: "Let us all love\nHiroshima, and its people."\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	17	17303	18752	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	I can believe it. Che never managed to numb\nhimself to other people's pain.\n
1	en	157	0x9d5c0	40284	18	18975	20089	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0240_000_0	19	That's why people loved him. And why he died.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	0				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	That bandanna you're always wearing - that thing's\na real antique.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	1				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Ever think about getting a new one?\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	2				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	This one's fine.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	3				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Come on, we can't have our Boss wearing a\nraggedy old thing like that...\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	4				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	It was a gift, OK? It wouldn't be right to get rid of\nit.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	5				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	It was? OK, then... Speaking of which,\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	6				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Che supposedly had a black scarf he used to take\neverywhere with him.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	7				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	A scarf?\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	8				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	One of his comrades gave it to him when he broke\nhis arm in battle.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	9				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Che used that silk scarf as a sling...\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	10				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	and the comrade who gave it to him became his\nsecond wife.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	11				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Aleida, right?\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	12				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	Even after his arm healed up, he never went\nanywhere without that scarf.\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	13				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	What about you? You get that bandanna from\nsomeone special?\n
1	en	157	0x9dc70	40391	14				v_bri_kaz0260_00	15	No, nothing like that. It's... important to me, that's all.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	0				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	You were saying Japan has a peace constitution,\ntoo?\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	1				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Yep. Japan renounced war in Article 9 of its\nconstitution.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	2				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Aspiring sincerely to an international peace based\non justice and order...\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	3				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	...the Japanese people forever renounce war as a\nsovereign right of the nation\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	4				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	and the threat or use of force as means of\nsettling international disputes.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	5				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	In order to accomplish the aim of the preceding\nparagraph,\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	6				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	land, sea, and air forces, as well as other war\npotential, will never be maintained.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	7				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	The right of belligerency of the state will not be\nrecognized.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	8				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	You know it by heart?\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	9				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Some things you never forget.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	10				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Impressive. There were some parts I didn't quite\nget, though\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	11				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	- like the whole "means of settling conflicts" thing.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	12				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Yeah, that's been the subject of debate.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	13				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Whether or not it forbids any and all use of force.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	14				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	The current constitution was originally drawn up\nunder the Allied occupation.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	15				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	The first draft was even submitted by Allied GHQ.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	16				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	So naturally there are some who feel the\nconstitution was imposed on Japan by foreigners.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	17				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	Things like that are never simple. Same for every\ncountry, I guess...\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	18				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	One thing's for sure, though\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	19				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	- not having an army let Japan focus on economic\nrecovery after the war.\n
1	en	158	0x9e190	40473	20				v_bri_kaz0300_000_0	21	In that sense, it's the same as Costa Rica.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	0	473	1407	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	Kaz, you were in the Japanese Self-Defense Force,\nright?\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	1	1462	2117	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	I thought that Article 9 you were talking about\nearlier\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	2	2117	2772	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	prevented Japan from having an army...\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	3	2786	3650	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	Yeah, and that's another subject of controversy.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	4	3678	4834	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	"The Self-Defense Force is an organization for the\npurpose of defense.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	5	4917	6199	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	Article 9 of the constitution does not deny Japan's\nright to self-defense.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	6	6339	7202	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	Therefore, the SDF is constitutional."\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	7	7244	7829	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	That's Tokyo's official stance.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	8	8108	8637	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	...Sounds complicated.\n
1	en	158	0x9e950	40597	9	8637	9877	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0300_010_0	10	Call it what you will, it's a distinctly Japanese way\nof interpreting things.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	0					11	The U.S. has military forces stationed in Japan,\nright?\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	1					11	Right. Even though the Allied occupation ended,\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	2					11	American forces are still stationed there under the\nU.S.-Japan Security Treaty.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	3					11	Unlike Costa Rica.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	4					11	Part of it stems from the occupation,\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	5					11	but Japan also occupies a key strategic position\nfrom America's perspective.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	6					11	It's next door to the Soviet Union, and close to\nChina and North Korea as well.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	7					11	It's integral to the security of Pacific Asia.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	8					11	For America, Japan represents a barrier against\ncommunism.\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	9					11	What about for Japan?\n
1	en	158	0x9ecf0	40655	10					11	As you might expect, there's deep-rooted\nopposition to the alliance.\n
