1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	0	473	1727	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	So tell me, Kaz, how's the Self-Defense Force you\nwere in different from an army?\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	1	1727	2563	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	The JSDF is set up for national defense.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	2	2633	3566	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	It's not configured for aggression against other\ncountries.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	3	3636	4360	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	It's what you'd call exclusive defense.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	4	4374	4834	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Exclusive... defense?\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	5	4834	6436	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	It's the fundamental strategic posture of the JSDF.\nNo preemptive attacks.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	6	6492	7648	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Only the minimum defensive action necessary after\nan enemy attacks.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	7	7662	8400	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Anything defined as self defense, then.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	8	8414	9473	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	The JSDF originated as the National Police Reserve.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	9	9501	11020	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Back then, American forces stationed in Japan\nwere being sent over to fight in the Korean War.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	10	11117	11939	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	So in a sense, the NPR was filling a gap.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	11	11981	13054	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Police, huh... Reminds me of the Costa Rican Civil\nGuard.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	12	13054	14349	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	Yeah, exactly. They're also dispatched to provide\ndisaster relief.\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	13	14433	15130	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	The year before I joined, they had a hell of a time\n
1	en	160	0xa04e0	41038	14	15130	16063	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0330_000_0	15	trying to rescue some buses that got hit by\nlandslides.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	0				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	So why'd you quit the JSDF?\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	1				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Because I didn't have a reason to be in Japan\nanymore.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	2				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Reason?\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	3				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	My mom had died three years earlier, so I didn't\nhave to care for her anymore.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	4				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	With her gone, there was no point hanging around\nin Japan.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	5				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Yeah, but a man with your talents could have risen\npretty high in the ranks, I imagine.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	6				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	I don't know what they made of me. Could be it\nactually alienated me from the brass.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	7				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	And personally, I could never get used to the idea\nof exclusive defense.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	8				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Meaning?\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	9				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	On a strategic level,\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	10				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	I can see how a country could go with the\nexclusive defense model used by the JSDF,\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	11				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	and I've got nothing against my fellow soldiers who\nbelieve in it.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	12				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	On a tactical level, though, it just rubbed me the\nwrong way.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	13				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	To put it simply, I was itching for a real fight.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	14				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	I figured....\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	15				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	And I felt like as long as I was in the JSDF,\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	16				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	I'd never be able to stand shoulder to shoulder\nwith my father who was in the U.S. Army.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	17				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Seeing Yukio Mishima's suicide didn't help, either.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	18				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Yukio Mishima... the guy who wrote "The Temple of\nthe Golden Pavilion"?\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	19				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	The way he questioned the status quo hit home\nwith me.\n
1	en	160	0xa0a60	41126	20				v_bri_kaz0330_010_0	21	Not that I admired his vision or anything. But it did\nget me thinking, that's for sure.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	0	445	1198	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Don't forget to give the MSF staff assignments.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	1	1267	2173	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Where you place people will determine how Mother\nBase grows..\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	2	2187	2382	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Right.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	3	2396	3357	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	That goes for me, too. Assign me wherever you\nthink is best.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	4	3371	4193	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	You? I thought you were second-in-command.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	5	4207	4667	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Don't worry, I've got that covered.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	6	4667	6018	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	But MSF is still small, and I don't have the leisure\nof sitting around on my butt all day.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	7	6673	7244	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	I see your point, but...\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	8	7258	8442	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Not to brag or anything, but I'd kick ass at\nwhatever job you gave me.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	9	8456	9445	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Put me in the area you want to focus on\ndeveloping most.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	10	9473	10267	0x0e463a34	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	I'll take real good care of the staff there.\n
1	en	161	0xa11f0	41247	11	10267	10699	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_kaz0020_000_0	12	Easy, Kaz.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	0					36	March 10, 1945. 381,300 cluster bombs were\ndropped on Tokyo.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	1					36	Japanese houses at the time were almost all made\nof wood.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	2					36	In that single day, a third of Tokyo burned to the\nground.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	3					36	My mom lost her family and home in that raid.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	4					36	She had to move to Yokosuka and live with her\ncousin.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	5					36	The B-29s kept on coming, razing other major\ncities throughout Japan without mercy.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	6					36	Then, in August, they dropped atomic bombs on\nHiroshima and Nagasaki,\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	7					36	and Japan surrendered to the United States at last.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	8					36	After that, Yokosuka was flooded with American\nsoldiers come to occupy the country.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	9					36	My mother was still in her mid-teens, and she\nlearned from her cousin\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	10					36	how to survive in that town - by servicing the\ntroops.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	11					36	That's how she met my father. And how I was born.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	12					36	My father was an officer\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	13					36	serving with the Government Section of Allied GHQ,\nunder General Whitney.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	14					36	Whitney was known as MacArthur's shadow,\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	15					36	and my father rode his coattails to a pretty high\nrank.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	16					36	While he was in Japan, he treated my mom like a\nwife.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	17					36	After he went back to the States, though, she\nnever heard from him.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	18					36	I wasn't born until after he went back.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	19					36	My mom raised me as a single mother.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	20					36	She used the money my father left her to open a\nshop,\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	21					36	selling cigarettes and stuff to occupation troops.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	22					36	It was a decent living. But I didn't have citizenship.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	23					36	They're working to change the law now, but back\nthen, if your father was unknown,\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	24					36	you couldn't get onto the Japanese family register\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	25					36	- and in Japan, that means you can't be Japanese.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	26					36	So as I watched the American soldiers around\ntown, I said to myself:\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	27					36	I'm a son of America, the victor.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	28					36	My hair and eyes were different from the skinny,\ndowntrodden Japanese around me.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	29					36	I told myself I'd leave this country someday and\nreturn to my true homeland..\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	30					36	When I was around 10 years old, my mom fell ill,\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	31					36	pretty much leaving me to run the shop in her\nplace.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	32					36	One day, I found a single picture of my father in\nthe back of a drawer in the shop.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	33					36	I'd show it to the American soldiers who came in to\nthe store.\n
1	en	161	0xa15f0	41311	34					36	A few years went by before one of them told me\nwho he was.\n
