1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	3				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	I do not know how much success they've had.\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	4				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	You do not hear of anyone still operating mines\naround here.\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	5				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	Oh...\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	6				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	Generally speaking, people here are not too thrilled\nwith outside capital\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	7				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	coming in and taking away the country's natural\nresources.\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	8				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	Four years ago, a group of protestors hurled\nstones at the parliament building\n
1	en	191	0xbfcf0	49103	9				v_bri_mis_paz0250_000_0	10	when people found out the country had been\nbestowing mining rights to foreign companies.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	0	445	1783	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	They must have had to clear a considerable\namount of forest to open up that mine.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	1	2020	3092	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	No doubt. So much for any hiding places, too.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	2	3120	4207	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	And it is not just the forest. Without the proper\nprecautions,\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	3	4277	5113	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	the mine's waste water could pollute the\ngroundwater.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	4	5182	6339	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	And to top it all off, they're developing nuclear\nweapons there, too.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	5	6450	7328	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	How can people care so little about their own\ncountry?\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	6	7370	8038	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	...I'm not the one to ask about that.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	7	8038	8930	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	Well, you may have abandoned your country,\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	8	9055	9752	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	but you still treasure where you came from.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	9	10198	10504	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	You think so?\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	10	10518	11312	0x0e55d959	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	Sure. I know you do.\n
1	en	192	0xc00b0	49163	11	11424	13931	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_paz0260_000_0	12	Paz, you... I... Whatever you do, never stop loving\nyour country.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	0				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	Since it was founded, my country has not once\nbeen able to choose its own path.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	1				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	First Spain, then America. Over one hundred years\nof this...\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	2				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	I know.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	3				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	In the 19th century,\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	4				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	an American mercenary named William Walker\nseized power in Nicaragua.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	5				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	I'd heard he exploited rivalries between the political\nparties.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	6				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	Even after we expelled him,\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	7				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	the Americans sent in their Marines under the\npretext of quelling political unrest.\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	8				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	And the real reason?\n
1	en	192	0xc04e0	49230	9				v_bri_amd0010_000_0	10	To intimidate the government and thwart the\nconstruction of the Nicaragua Canal.\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	0				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	I thought the U.S. already has the rights to build a\ncanal in Nicaragua...\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	1				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	They never intended to build a canal there. They\nhad already started digging in Panama.\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	2				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	A canal in Nicaragua would break up the Panama\nCanal's monopoly,\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	3				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	reason enough for America to stick its nose into\nour affairs.\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	4				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	They snagged the rights so nobody else could\nbuild there...\n
1	en	192	0xc0880	49288	5				v_bri_amd0010_010_0	6	It was General Sandino who finally stood up to los\nyanquis.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	0				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	General Sandino waged a guerrilla war against the\nMarines,\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	1				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	at last driving them out. He was a true hero, and -\nto us Sandinistas - like a father.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	2				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	But when they pulled out, the Americans left us\nsomething to remember them by.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	3				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	The National Guard.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	4				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	Yes, la Guardia. In name, a peacekeeping force.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	5				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	But in reality, Commander Somoza's personal army,\nanswering to no one else.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	6				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	Unable to stay silent, General Sandino went to\nprotest...\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	7				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	and was assassinated by Somoza as he made his\nway home.\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	8				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	It was then that Somoza and los sapos\n
1	en	192	0xc0b40	49332	9				v_bri_amd0010_020_0	10	began to eat our country alive, from the inside.\n
1	en	192	0xc0f10	49393	0					1	It has been 40 years since the Somoza family\nbecame the rulers of Nicaragua.\n
1	en	192	0xc0f10	49393	1					1	With la Guardia at their command, no one can\nc�
