1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	0				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	The river that flows between Nicaragua and Costa\nRica is el Río San Juan.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	1				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	It connects Lago Cocibolca to el Mar Caribe.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	2				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	It is a gentle river, safe even for small boats. You\nhave to watch out for the sharks, though.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	3				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	Sharks? In a river?\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	4				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	El tiburón toro live in the river. But there's nothing\nto fear. San Juan will protect you always.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	5				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	Juan?\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	6				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	The Rio San Juan takes its name from San Juan,\nthe man who baptized Jesús.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	7				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	The man you call John the Baptist in your country.\n
1	en	194	0xc2170	49687	8				v_bri_amd0030_000_0	9	John, huh... I'll be careful anyway. I'd hate to drown\n- end up a John Doe.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	0				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	There is a volcanic island in Lago Cocibolca called\nOmetepe.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	1				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	It's formed from two connected volcanoes, shaped\nlike a porongo (gourd).\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	2				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	The one with smoke coming out of it?\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	3				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	That's the one. The smoking volcano is\nConcepción. It erupts once in a while.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	4				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	The other one is Maderas.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	5				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	It's never erupted in recorded history. There's a\nlake in the caldera at the top.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	6				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	A crater lake... Let's hope there's not another\nunderground factory inside...\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	7				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	Don't say that!\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	8				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	If Peace Walker's nukes detonated there, it would\ndestabilize the magma underneath.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	9				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	It could cause the volcano to erupt again.\n
1	en	194	0xc2510	49745	10				v_bri_amd0040_000_0	11	If that happens, my country is doomed. Snake, you\nmust stop Peace Walker!\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	0				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	We are Sandinistas, the heirs of General Sandino's\nwill.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	1				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Sandino... the father of modern guerrilla warfare.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	2				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	To you, maybe. To us, he's more like a real father.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	3				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	That why you named yourselves after him?\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	4				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Of course. La Frente Sandinista de Liberación\nNacional...\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	5				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	the Sandinista National Liberation Front is named\nin his honor.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	6				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	We share the General's goal - to take back our\ncountry's freedom.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	7				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	In his day, from America. In ours, from the despot\nSomoza.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	8				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Times have changed, but the song remains the\nsame.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	9				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	"Patria Libre o Morir"... That is our slogan.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	10				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	El Che used to say the same thing, you know?\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	11				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	"Patria o Muerte"... the slogan of the Cuban\nRevolution.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	12				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	So you're fighting for a socialist revolution, too,\nthen?\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	13				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	No. It's not about ideology. We only want to live\nnormal lives.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	14				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	We are sick of living in a country where you can\nbe beaten for walking down the street.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	15				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Where you never know if your neighbor is snitching\non you.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	16				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Our goal is to give back the country to its people.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	17				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Yeah. The Cuban Revolution started out like that,\ntoo...\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	18				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	We are few in number, but we will win.\n
1	en	194	0xc2950	49813	19				v_bri_amd0050_000_0	20	Victoria o muerte. For a revolutionary, there is no\nother fate.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	0				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	Augusto César Sandino... general and hero of\nNicaragua.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	1				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	Fifty years ago, conflict broke out between the\npolitical parties of Nicaragua.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	2				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The U.S. Marines intervened in the name of\nrestoring order.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	3				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The only one who refused to listen to their call for\nreconciliation was General Sandino.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	4				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	One man against the Marines... That takes guts.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	5				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The General wasn't a professional soldier. Neither\nwere his compadres.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	6				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	And they certainly could not match the Marines'\nequipment.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	7				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	But he used the land to his advantage, lurking in\nthe fields and hills of Nicaragua,\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	8				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	and using surprise attacks to harass the Marines.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	9				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	I know. I've read plenty of textbooks on guerrilla\nwarfare.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	10				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	In the end, he drove them out. He was truly a role\nmodel for us.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	11				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	Yeah, but that was the '30s - didn't the Depression\nhave something to do with it?\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	12				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	It takes cash to wage war.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	13				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	Perhaps. But there is no denying what the General\naccomplished.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	14				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The people hailed him as the "General de Hombres\nLibres" - the general of free men.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	15				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	They loved him. And then...\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	16				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	He was assassinated - by Anastasio Somoza\nGarcia, commander of la Guardia.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	17				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The father of the current Somoza.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	18				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	Somoza had the Americans' backing. That is the\nway things were.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	19				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	The General may be dead, and the times may have\nchanged,\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	20				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	but his will lives on inside each of us.\n
1	en	195	0xc3080	49928	21				v_bri_amd0060_000_0	22	We are the sons and daughters of General Sandino.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	0				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Hey. How's the watermelon business these days?\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	1				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Not bad. I hear the compas back home are hanging\nin there, too.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	2				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Good to hear.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	3				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	...You know our nickname. I'm impressed.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	4				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	No wonder they call you Boss.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	5				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	You used to hide your weapons in hollowed-out\nwatermelons,\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	6				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	smuggle 'em right past the National Guard.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	7				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	You even transported pineapple grenades that\nway.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	8				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	The name caught on among sympathizers in the\nregion and...\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	9				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	What are you talking about?\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	10				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Huh?\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	11				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	They call us watermelon sellers because the\nGeneral's name\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	12				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	sounds like the word for watermelon, sandía.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	13				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	...Really?\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	14				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Si.\n
1	en	195	0xc38b0	50059	15				v_bri_amd0070_000_0	16	Dammit, Kaz...\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	0					13	A revolution to bring peace to your country, huh.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	1					13	No matter how much we fight, peace never gets\nany closer, does it?\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	2					13	I do not like it, but it is the only way.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	3					13	If we do not fight back, they'll just keep exploiting\nus.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	4					13	You want proof? Look at our country's history.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	5					13	Revolution is a means and not an end, I know.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	6					13	When the revolution is victorious, I want my\ncountry to be at peace.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	7					13	But to bring peace, we have to first eliminate that\ntraitorous scum.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	8					13	It's an old saying: "We make war that we may live\nin peace."\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	9					13	Who said it?\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	10					13	Aristotle.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	11					13	You know your history.\n
1	en	195	0xc3d40	50132	12					13	I had a good teacher.\n
