1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	0				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Did you see a quetzal, Snake?\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	1				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Yep.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	2				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	What do you think? Did it look like a snake?\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	3				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Huh? No, it didn't look like a snake.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	4				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Oh, really? ...Must be different from the\nquetzalcoatl, then.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	5				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Quetzalcoatl?\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	6				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	A winged snake from Mayan and Aztec legend.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	7				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	A winged snake...\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	8				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Weird, right? I bet it's a UMA.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	9				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Amanda and everybody says Quetzalcoatl is a\nquetzal in the form of a god.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	10				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	But there's no way anybody'd mistake a snake for\na bird,\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	11				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	I think the legend of Quetzalcoatl came first.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	12				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Somebody saw it and adopted it as their god,\nbefore they saw the quetzal.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	13				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	After that, somebody saw a bird that looked like\nthe image of the god,\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	14				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	and so they named it "quetzal."\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	15				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	You mean it happened the other way around?\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	16				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	If you ask me, the quetzalcoatl was probably a\npterosaur that survived.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	17				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	I mean, it's got wings, and it's a reptile, so it\nprobably looks kind of like a snake, right?\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	18				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Pterosaurs live on in Africa even today. They're\ncalled the Kongamato and the Olitiau.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	19				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	So it makes sense that there'd be pterosaurs on\nthe American continent, too,\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	20				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	and that they survived until the Mayan and Aztec\neras.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	21				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Lucky for us they're not still around today.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	22				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	Who says they aren't? The dinosaurs supposedly\ndied out 65 million years ago,\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	23				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	and the Aztec civilization only arose about 600\nyears ago.\n
1	en	211	0xd3750	54133	24				v_bri_chc0090_000_0	25	If they managed to survive 65 million years, surely\nthey couldn't be wiped out in 600.\n
1	en	212	0xd43b0	54331	0	445	1922	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0100_000_0	14	A base disguised as a mine? We never made it\nthat far.\n
