1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	4	3775	4096	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	I guess so...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	5	4123	5851	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	But that was the day before Peace Day. Paz was\ngoing to sing in a band. I couldn't wait to hear her.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	6	5907	7397	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	What if she wasn't going to double cross them...\nmaybe she just wanted to at least be able to enjoy\nthat...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	7	7425	7718	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Chico...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	8	7746	8818	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	I knew it... If only I hadn't run away...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	9	8832	9835	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	If I'd talked to her... promised I wouldn't tell\nanybody...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	10	9849	11270	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Don't second-guess yourself. Think about who\nmight have been hurt if you'd kept quiet.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	11	11298	11563	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Yeah, but...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	12	11577	12524	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Besides, we don't know for sure she's dead.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	13	12552	13709	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	She was ejected into the ocean. And she had\nscuba equipment with her.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	14	13736	13973	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Yeah...\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	15	13987	15450	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	Stop beating yourself up over the past, Chico - put\nyour hope in the future.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	16	15478	16676	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	The past is the past, but the future is up to you.\n
1	en	219	0xdb190	56089	17	16732	17136	0x0e559953	v_bri_chc0130_010_0	18	...Right, Boss.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	0	501	1365	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	"AI" stands for artificial intelligence.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	1	1448	2479	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	It refers to a manmade machine possessing an\nintellect on par\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	2	2479	2883	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	with that of a human being.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	3	2883	3287	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	Is that even possible?\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	4	3315	4151	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	Possible? You've seen it with your own eyes.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	5	4249	4973	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	What the AI weapons are capable of.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	6	4987	5935	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	How Peace Walker carried out The Boss' will in the\nend.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	7	5990	7105	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	No mere machine could ever choose to sacrifice\nitself like that.\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	8	7161	8317	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	I just can't see how a bunch of circuits and\nswitches could have a will of its own...\n
1	en	219	0xdb7c0	56188	9	8373	9780	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0010_000_1	10	It's called The Ghost in the Machine, Snake... And it\nexists.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	0					20	Let's say, hypothetically, there really was an\nintelligent machine.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	1					20	How would you prove it?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	2					20	What's the difference between a high-level\ncomputer and an AI?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	3					20	It depends on what you choose to define as\nintelligence...\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	4					20	For example, Snake, listen to my voice talking to\nyou now. Suppose it were an AI?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	5					20	Say what?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	6					20	What if the living, breathing me, sitting here\ndrinking tea and talking to you,\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	7					20	were actually an AI, programmed to simulate my\nthoughts and my voice?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	8					20	You're nuts.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	9					20	No, I'm not. You couldn't prove I was a real human\nbeing, could you?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	10					20	It'd be... No.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	11					20	Hm. So if an AI is so advanced you can't tell it\nfrom the real thing,\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	12					20	wouldn't you call that intelligence?\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	13					20	I'm not quite convinced.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	14					20	But that's only one way to look at it.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	15					20	It's roughly how Dr. Turing explained artificial\nintelligence.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	16					20	The test I described is called the Turing Test in his\nhonor.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	17					20	Yeah, well, it sounds like a bad joke to me.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	18					20	That singing monster of yours put me through hell.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	19					20	If I never meet one again, it'll be too soon.\n
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	20					20	Shame. And I was about to let an AI take my place\nin our conversation.�a�ͤ��߭G �dY���H�rU{�♾����|P.�
1	en	219	0xdbb50	56245	21					20	�ͤ��߭G �dY���H�rU{�♾����|P.�
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	0				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Dr. Alan Turing is known as the father of computer\nscience.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	1				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	It was he who formulated the basic theory of AI.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	2				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	During the war, he worked for GC&CS.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	3				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	The government's cryptology school... the one that\nbroke Enigma.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	4				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	The very same.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	5				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Without him, they might never have been able to\ndecipher the Germans' code.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	6				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	...But then they arrested him.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	7				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Why? Weren't his some of the greatest\ncontributions to winning the war?\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	8				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Homosexuality was a crime in Britain back then.\nAnd he was gay.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	9				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	I know exactly how crushed he was. I feel it's my\nduty to further his AI research...\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	10				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Like "The Imitation Game," huh?\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	11				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Ah, you know it, then?\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	12				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	The clue that inspired the Turing Test came from\na trifling little party game.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	13				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	You exchange written notes with another person\nbehind a screen,\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	14				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	and try to guess their gender whether they're a\nwoman,\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	15				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	or a man pretending to be a woman....\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	16				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	It's surprisingly difficult to tell the difference. Man\nor woman, human or machine...\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	17				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	What do the differences even mean?\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	18				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Dr. Turing was fascinated by the differences\nbetween men and women.\n
1	en	220	0xdc290	56361	19				v_bri_dsl0020_010_0	20	Perhaps that's what led him to formulate his test.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	0	487	1421	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	What if you hadn't used The Boss as the model for\nthe AI?\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	1	1434	1685	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	What do you mean?\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	2	1713	3078	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	If the end goal is MAD, seems like there are more\nsuitable models out there.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	3	3190	4569	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	A military chief of staff, or a nuclear technician...\neven the President.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	4	4709	5837	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Patriotism, leadership, good judgment...\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	5	5962	7760	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Each of those qualities is essential when deciding\nwhether to launch a nuke, and she had them all.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	6	8066	8470	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Wouldn't the President, too?\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	7	8484	9557	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	It's not enough. She had the will for it.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	8	9599	9835	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Will?\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	9	9877	11270	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Let me explain, Snake. Peace Walker is a\nstand-alone weapon.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	10	11368	12371	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	It has to decide on its own whether or not to\nlaunch a nuke.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	11	12455	13165	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	It can't turn to anyone else for advice.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	12	13207	14781	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	It can't do its job if it's not prepared to take sole\nresponsibility for the fate of the world.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	13	14879	15840	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	I know of no other human being who could bear\nthat burden.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	14	15868	16091	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	...That's it?\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	15	16133	17818	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	There were technical considerations, of course. To\nbegin with, the female brain\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	16	17902	18543	0x0ebd8542	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	is a more appropriate model for an AI.\n
1	en	220	0xdc990	56473	17	18543	19365	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_dsl0030_000_1	18	Wait...the female brain?\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	0					22	Is it really necessary to make machines think like\nhumans?\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	1					22	I'd think empathy would get in the way of making\nthe decision to retaliate.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	2					22	I admit, human thought may not be fully compatible\nwith level-headed decision making.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	3					22	Then again, is there such a thing as non-human\nthought?\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	4					22	The moment computers became so advanced\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	5					22	that they could calculate faster than human\nbeings, a new dream was born.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	6					22	Dream?\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	7					22	If we can create a machine that can do advanced\nmathematics,\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	8					22	why can't we create one that can think like a\nhuman?\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	9					22	The 19th century author Auguste Villiers\nl'Isle-Adam wrote a book called "Tomorrow's Eve"\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	10					22	in which the protagonist fashions an artificial\nwoman to serve as his ideal lover...\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	11					22	I believe the idea of creating an intelligent being\nwith our own hands\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	12					22	is one of mankind's oldest dreams.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	13					22	So AI is a product of people's dreams then.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	14					22	Not only AI. Civilization itself\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	15					22	is the process by which mankind gives form to its\ndreams.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	16					22	But not all dreams are pleasant.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	17					22	Some have expressed fears about "non-human\nintelligence."\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	18					22	Yeah, they want the benefits with none of the risks.\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	19					22	Some fear that intelligent robots might rise up and\nrebel against their human masters,\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	20					22	as portrayed by the Czech writer Karel Capek in\nhis "Rossum's Universal Robots."\n
1	en	221	0xdda20	56738	21					22	Or in the 1970 film "Colossus: The Forbin Project,"\n
