1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	10				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	I never said such a thing. Of course my work will\ncontinue...\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	11				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	Here?\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	12				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	In my eyes, the growth of MSF\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	13				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	as an organization is analogous to the development\nof the nervous system.\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	14				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	The way you become intertwined, stimulate each\nother,\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	15				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	branch out in new directions... It's really quite\ninspirational.\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	16				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	Interesting way to put it.\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	17				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	Of course, I intend to repay you for your\nhospitality.\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	18				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	The fruits of my research will be at your disposal.\n
1	en	227	0xe3aa0	58282	19				v_bri_dsl0170_000_0	20	Why not assign me to the R&D team? I promise you\nwon't be disappointed.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	0				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	You know, that ID card I got from Huey wouldn't\nlet me into the lab.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	1				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Of course not. I deactivated it.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	2				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Why?\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	3				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	As though I'd let him into my lab!\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	4				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	You don't seem to care for him much. Even though\nyou two are in the same field.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	5				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Oh, please. For starters, I can't stand that\nwishy-washy attitude of his.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	6				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	If he's got something to say to me, he should be a\nman and come say it in person,\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	7				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	not write some furtive letter.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	8				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	But if his card doesn't work, how's he supposed to...\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	9				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	And another thing! The way he always demeans\nhimself.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	10				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Every other sentence begins with "I could be\nwrong, but..."\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	11				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Why can't he just do what he does and be\nconfident about it?!\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	12				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Well, there was the thing about his dissertation\nbeing plagiarized...\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	13				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	The real problem is, he doesn't have a whit of\nindependence in him.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	14				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	In Costa Rica, and even back at NASA he was\nalways following people around\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	15				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	asking if there was some way he could help.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	16				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Why doesn't he stop his whining, take a stand, and\nface up to me?\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	17				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	I think I know how he feels....\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	18				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	And to top it all off, he seems to enjoy referring to\nhimself by that nickname I gave him.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	19				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	I simply don't get it. How can he be so submissive?\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	20				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	So Huey was a nickname you gave him...\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	21				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	That's right. I took it from the name of a drone in\nthe science fiction film "Silent Running."\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	22				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Drone?\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	23				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	It's a type of worker robot.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	24				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	The way he follows people, just waiting for them to\nboss him around, is so similar. I couldn't help myself.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	25				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Oh.. It's an American film directed by Douglas\nTrumbull,\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	26				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	the man who did the special effects for "2001: A\nSpace Odyssey."\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	27				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Imagine that. A scientist who likes science fiction...\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	28				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	And that's not all!\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	29				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	There's more?\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	30				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	He's a man!\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	31				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	...Hang on, I seem to remember you saying he\nshould be more of a man.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	32				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	...Yes, well, he is an awfully good scientist.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	33				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Without his Reptile Pod, my Mammal Pod wouldn't\nfunction.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	34				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Just as the cerebrum can't exist without the\ncerebellum and the brain stem.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	35				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	He did a fine job of designing it, I'll give him that.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	36				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	That sounded suspiciously like praise...\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	37				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Hmph. It's merely an objective observation.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	38				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	I like people who possess the will to move forward.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	39				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	I have no time for those who refuse to take\ncontrol of their lives.\n
1	en	228	0xe4140	58388	40				v_bri_dsl0180_000_0	41	Until he does, my opinion remains the same.\n
1	en	228	0xe4f40	58612	0					1	So...you and Cécile seemed to be enjoying\nyourselves back at that lab.\n
