1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	0				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	You said you could do a monkey call.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	1				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Yes! Want to hear it?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	2				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Yeah, I won't be able to get it off my mind until I do.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	3				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Alright. Here it is, then.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	4				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	...That's pretty good.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	5				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	I can do others, too. Like a sheep, a pig...\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	6				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Yeah?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	7				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Wow. What about a pig?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	8				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	That's uncanny!\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	9				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Ha ha. I can do a rabbit as well.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	10				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	A rabbit? Rabbits make noises?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	11				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Certainly. But they're very faint,\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	12				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	so perhaps only someone who has owned one\nwould recognize them.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	13				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	I see. So what do they sound like?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	14				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Listen very carefully.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	15				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	OK.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	16				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Seriously? Rabbits sound like that?\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	17				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	I assure you, they do. Listen again.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	18				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	Never heard a rabbit do that before.\n
1	en	233	0xe9630	59747	19				v_bri_ccl0160_000_0	20	They do, I swear!\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	0					19	Costa Rica is the home to many hummingbirds as\nwell.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	1					19	The people here call them colibri.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	2					19	Colibri, huh...\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	3					19	Hummingbirds are the world's smallest birds.\nAlthough they can vary from species to species,\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	4					19	most are around 10 cm in length, and the smallest\nno more than five.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	5					19	It's amazing to think that something so small can\nstill be a fully developed bird.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	6					19	I'd think a bird that tiny would get mistaken for a\nbug.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	7					19	Actually they are often mistaken for sphinx moths.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	8					19	The little darlings feed on flower nectar,\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	9					19	and their beaks are long and thin to help them\ndrink up the nectar.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	10					19	They beat their wings far faster than any other\nbird.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	11					19	The smallest ones over 70 times a second! Can\nyou imagine?\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	12					19	What for?\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	13					19	They hover in midair while they suck their nectar.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	14					19	They can even fly straight backwards.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	15					19	Hovering... like a helicopter!\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	16					19	That's why Amanda and her unit called that\nchopper colibri.\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	17					19	I think it is an insult to the birds to give their\nnames to weapons, don't you?\n
1	en	233	0xe9b60	59830	18					19	Don't ask me. Ask the birds how they feel about it.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	0				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Have you heard of the manakins?\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	1				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Never.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	2				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Unbelievable! You came all the way to Costa Rica\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	3				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	and you've never even heard of them!?\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	4				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	I didn't come here to look at...\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	5				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Manakins are known for their beautiful courtship\ndances.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	6				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	The Orange-collared Manakin is said to live on the\nPacific side,\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	7				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	the White-bearded Manakin on the Caribbean side,\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	8				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	and the Long-tailed Manakin is in the central basin.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	9				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	The Long-tailed Manakins are especially distinctive.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	10				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	What's special about them?\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	11				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	First, each young male picks an older male to\nteach him how to dance.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	12				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Then teachers and pupils all get in a group and\ndance for the females.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	13				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	However, only the teachers get paired off with\nfemales.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	14				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	The pupils practice their technique for seven years\nbefore striking out on their own\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	15				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	and finding their own pupils.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	16				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	I guess it takes time to get good at anything,\nwhether it's dancing or soldiering.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	17				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Manakins always have plenty to eat.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	18				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Apparently that is why they have so much free\ntime to practice dancing.\n
1	en	234	0xea1f0	59935	19				v_bri_ccl0120_000_0	20	Unlike us, obviously...\n
1	en	234	0xea890	60041	0	445	1852	0x0ec8dda0	v_bri_ccl0130_000_0	17	Another well-known bird of the cloud forest is the\nThree-wattled Bellbird.\n
1	en	234	0xea890	60041	1	1894	2730	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_ccl0130_000_0	17	Wattled? What the hell's a wattle?\n
1	en	234	0xea890	60041	2	3092	3705	0x0ec8dda0	v_bri_ccl0130_000_0	17	It is just like your beard, Snake!\n
1	en	234	0xea890	60041	3	3761	3914	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_ccl0130_000_0	17	What?\n
1	en	234	0xea890	60041	4	3970	4931	0x0ec8dda0	v_bri_ccl0130_000_0	17	It's a piece of flesh that hangs down from its chin.\n
