1	en	239	0xef830	61315	0				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Tell me, Snake, do you ever put sweets in your\nrations?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	1				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Never thought about it before. Why?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	2				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	I simply love them! French sweets are très délicieux.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	3				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Everyone knows crêpes, but there are also\nprofiteroles,\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	4				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	éclairs, madeleines, financiers...\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	5				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Then there's Tarte Tatins, and you can't forget\nsavarins.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	6				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Oh, and mille-feuilles, crêpes, blancmanges...\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	7				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	You already mentioned crêpes.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	8				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Soufflés, croquembouches, canelés, floretins, kouign\namanns, Peach Melbas...\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	9				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Finished?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	10				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	...And macarons! I love those the most.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	11				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Macarons parisiens are the best.They're so cute\nand colorful,\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	12				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	and they contain meringue, so they melt right in\nyour mouth.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	13				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Wow. I'd no idea macaroons were that popular in\nFrance, too.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	14				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	What do you mean "in France, too"?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	15				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Macaroons are those coconut-flavored cookies,\nright?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	16				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Excuse you! Macarons contains almond powder, not\ncoconuts!\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	17				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Don't they have peanuts in them? I've had them in\nJapan a few times.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	18				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	I thought they were called "makorons."\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	19				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Makarons? Some cheap imitation, I am sure.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	20				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	French macarons have a long and distinguished\nhistory.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	21				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	They date back to the 16th century, when\nCatherine de' Medici of Florence\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	22				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	married into the French royal family.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	23				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	The story goes that her pâtissiers shared the\nrecipe after they arrived.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	24				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	That's a history of over 500 years.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	25				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	So... they're originally from Italy, then.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	26				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Well...\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	27				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Don't macaroons come from Italy, too?\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	28				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Look, I do not really think...\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	29				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Keep in mind "macaron" is almost identical to\n"macaroni."\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	30				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Well, that settles it.\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	31				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	...I cannot believe this. To associate macarons with\nmacaroni!\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	32				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	You, Monsieur Miller, are an insensible oaf!\n
1	en	239	0xef830	61315	33				v_bri_ccl0210_000_0	34	Hey, hey, why am I the bad guy? Come on, Cécile,\nwait! Cécile!\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	0				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Hello! Snake, mon chéri.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	1				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	You been drinking, Cécile?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	2				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Can't you please do something about this pest?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	3				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Who?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	4				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Monsieur Miller.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	5				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	What's he done now...\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	6				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Wine.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	7				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Wine?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	8				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	He said he thought the soldiers might tire of\nhaving beer all the time,\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	9				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	so he's brought in some wine instead. And then he\ngave me a bunch.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	10				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	That was... nice of him.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	11				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	But the past two years have been bad for French\nwine.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	12				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Perhaps it is the bad weather we have had, but\nwhatever the case,\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	13				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	I told him the last thing you want to be drinking\nright now is French wine.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	14				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	California and Chile have good wine, too.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	15				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	But then he goes and stocks the pantry with\nnothing but French wine from '72.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	16				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	The stuff is a disaster.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	17				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Then he says, "Come on, Cécile, have a taste of\nthe old country."\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	18				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	I had a terrible feeling about it, so I decided to just\ntaste it at first.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	19				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	And then again, and then a few more times.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	20				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Took one for the team, huh?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	21				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	But when you do a tasting, don't you usually spit\nthe wine out?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	22				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	But bottle after bottle, nothing but garbage,\nabsolute garbage.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	23				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Not one half-decent bottle in the whole lot! "Taste\nof the old country" he says...\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	24				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Sorry to hear that.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	25				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	I cannot believe this! What will we do with all this\nwine!\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	26				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	The guys aren't too picky when it comes to taste.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	27				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	You sound just like Monsieur Miller.\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	28				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Huh?\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	29				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	When I complained, he simply said, "As long as it\ngets the job done."\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	30				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	You people are such culinary savages!\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	31				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	Allez tous vous faire voir ! ...Aaaah, somebody take\nme back to Paris! Please!\n
1	en	240	0xf0320	61490	32				v_bri_ccl0190_000_0	33	...I think you've had a little too much, Cécile.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	0					8	You seem like a very... how do I put this...\nuninhibited woman.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	1					8	You think so? I am no different from other Parisian\nwomen. Not since May 1968.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	2					8	May 1968... The general strike that almost brought\ndown the president?\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	3					8	Right. But it was more than just a strike.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	4					8	It started with the student movement at\nStrasbourg University in '66.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	5					8	They did not want anything from the country, but\ninstead sought reform at the school.\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	6					8	That helped ignite a fire in the hearts of scores of\ndissatisfied young people,\n
1	en	240	0xf0d60	61654	7					8	and the movement spread all over France.\n
