1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	0	459	2020	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	Doc, I'm starting to sense your feelings about\nnukes are a little different from your colleagues.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	1	2048	2326	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	How do you mean?\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	2	2340	3009	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	Your little chat with Coldman back there.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	3	3023	4472	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	I...told you my father worked on the Manhattan\nProject, right?\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	4	4625	5015	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	You're familiar with it?\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	5	5085	6213	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	The basics, yeah. It was the project that kicked off\nthe nuclear age.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	6	6227	7300	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	That's right. Some of the finest minds of the 20th\ncentury,\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	7	7300	8317	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	including multiple Nobel Prize winners, worked on it.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	8	8359	9808	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	They spent over two billion dollars - and that's in\n1940's money.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	9	9822	11605	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	It culminated in the successful nuclear test at\nAlamogordo on July 16, 1945.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	10	11605	12566	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	And the first use in combat a few weeks later.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	11	12873	13082	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	Doc?\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	12	13096	13444	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	...I can't walk.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	13	13597	15074	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	My spine isn't shaped like everybody else's. I can't\nmove my lower body.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	14	15088	16063	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	So I've never taken a single step, not since I was\nborn.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	15	16077	16983	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	What's that got to do with your dad being in the\nManhattan Project?\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	16	16983	18543	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	As a result of his research, my father was exposed\nto high doses of radiation.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	17	18640	19658	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	It can't be a coincidence that my chromosomes are\nmessed up.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	18	19964	20243	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	That's...\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	19	20271	21524	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	That's why I didn't want for these nukes to be\nanother sword of Damocles.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	20	21538	22764	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	I wanted to give them a real purpose: to be a\ndeterrent against war.\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	21	22778	22987	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	Doc...\n
1	en	241	0xf15f0	61791	22	23001	24520	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0010_000_0	23	And Coldman... We have to stop him. Whatever it\ntakes.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	0					7	August 6, 1945 - Hiroshima. August 9 - Nagasaki.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	1					7	The atom bomb attacks on Japan.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	2					7	My father was married to his work.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	3					7	I don't have a single memory of going places or\ndoing stuff with him on weekends.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	4					7	But even as a kid I knew on some level that it was\nfor his family - I respected him.\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	5					7	My mother used to tell me all the time, "Your\nfather is a brilliant scientist."\n
1	en	241	0xf1dc0	61916	6					7	"He saved countless American boys from death."\n
