1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	0	445	1490	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	Peace Walker is fitted with several close-range\nweapons.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	1	1504	2758	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	We'd also planned for it to be able to enter enemy\nterritory and self-detonate.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	2	2814	3761	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	Wonderful. What exactly are we dealing with here?\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	3	3803	5154	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	Well, the flamethrowers, for one thing - One in the\nfront, and one in the rear.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	4	5210	6952	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	Then there are the S-mines. They're like cluster\nbombs. It scatters them from its leg hatches.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	5	6979	7787	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	If you see those open, you'd better clear out\nquickly.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	6	7815	9278	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	And finally, the rockets. These can travel quite a\ndistance, so stay sharp.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	7	9306	10323	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	On the other hand, it can't fire them from too\nclose...\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	8	10379	11131	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	but then again there are those flamethrowers.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	9	11173	12343	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	It can also use its legs as weapons against any\ninfantry on the ground.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	10	12357	12538	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	Ouch.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	11	12552	13681	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	But remember, Peace Walker technically isn't\ncomplete.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	12	13736	15046	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	With just the Reptile Pod, it can only perform\nrelatively simple maneuvers.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	13	15116	15715	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	They've got certain quirks too.\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	14	15770	16370	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	What's more, look carefully\n
1	en	248	0xf87b0	63611	15	16370	17401	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_mis_huy0035_000_0	16	and you should be able to predict which attack is\ncoming next.\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	0					9	Snake! You have to stop Peace Walker from\nlaunching that nuke!\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	1					9	Calm down. What do I need to do?\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	2					9	To tell you the truth, I don't really know...\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	3					9	The Mammal Pod has probably enhanced both its\nthought patterns and mobility.\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	4					9	The S-mines, rockets, flamethrowers - they're all\nthe same, but\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	5					9	I don't know what else to say...\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	6					9	You'll just have to stay sharp and watch for how it\nbehaves before each of the attacks.\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	7					9	I'll give it a shot. There's no time for anything else.\n
1	en	248	0xf8da0	63706	8					9	Sorry I can't be more helpful. You can do it, Snake!\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	0	445	1504	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	OK, but why does Peace Walker need to walk on\ntwo legs?\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	1	1560	2117	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	I'd think treads would be good enough.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	2	2131	2633	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	You'd be wrong.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	3	2661	3845	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	As you know, the terrain in Central America is\nrugged and complex.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	4	3873	4764	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	Especially along the Caribbean coast of Costa Rica.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	5	4778	5753	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	You've got jungles, swamps, mountains...\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	6	5767	7147	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	And the only way to get across all that terrain, no\nmatter how rugged, is on legs.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	7	7216	8205	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	But wouldn't it be tough to cross a swamp even on\nlegs?\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	8	8233	8888	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	It wasn't easy, that's for sure.\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	9	8930	9668	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	The reason we picked Costa Rica as a proving\nground\n
1	en	249	0xf9120	63762	10	9668	10644	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0100_000_0	11	is because we can test it on every type of terrain.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	0	459	2061	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	This "Peace Walker Project" the CIA's talking about\n- you believed in it, huh?\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	1	2089	3678	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	Yeah. I believed... Actually, maybe I just wanted to\nbelieve.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	2	3691	4012	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	What do you mean?\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	3	4026	5614	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	Here's how Coldman explained it. Peace Walker's a\nweapon for peace,\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	4	5656	6548	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	one to ensure true nuclear deterrence.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	5	6589	7606	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	It'd be the anchor to bring stability to Central\nAmerica...\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	6	7620	9195	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	He told me, "The nuclear weapons system you've\nbuilt will never be used.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	7	9209	10323	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	It will forever stand vigil as an icon of peace."\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	8	10337	11647	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	And to achieve that, he needed my bipedal\nlocomotion technology...\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	9	11772	12399	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	To be honest, I was flattered.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	10	12413	12733	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	Flattered?\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	11	12747	13137	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	Wouldn't you be?\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	12	13165	14363	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	My colleagues in the scientific community have\nnever taken me seriously.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	13	14391	15868	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	They told me bipedal locomotion was a pipe dream,\nthat it'd never amount to anything.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	14	15882	16815	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	It was the first time anybody recognized my work...\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	15	16885	17944	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	How could I not be happy? And besides...\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	16	17944	18724	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	It was my chance to surpass my father.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	17	18836	20131	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	To create nukes for peace... Or so I thought,\nanyway.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	18	20131	20828	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	But Coldman's really going through with it.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	19	20828	21775	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	Yeah. Makes perfect sense, really.\n
1	en	249	0xf9500	63824	20	21873	23684	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_000_0	21	No one would give the project any notice unless\nthey could prove that an unmanned system is\ncapable of launching a nuke.\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	0	445	1100	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	Why is Coldman gonna launch a nuke?\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	1	1100	2173	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	If all he wants to do is prove the AI retaliation\nsystem works,\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	2	2187	2939	0x0e28dce6	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	he doesn't need to put a live warhead on it.\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	3	2939	4277	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	I agree. He could demonstrate the system by\nlaunching the missile alone\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	4	4277	5349	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	- without a warhead. I asked him the same thing at\nfirst.\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	5	5363	6213	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	He said there was no point in using a dummy\nmissile...\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	6	6283	7592	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	That it'd take an actual nuclear launch to deter\npotential enemies.\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	7	7592	8860	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	Launching the real deal gives him a leg up in\nnegotiations with Langley.\n
1	en	249	0xf9c50	63941	8	8902	9445	0x0ea8e31c	v_bri_huy0110_010_0	9	That's what he's really after.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	0				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	How'd you get your hands on Granin's research?\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	1				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	That was also Coldman. He used his Agency\ncontacts to get a hold of it.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	2				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Giving you stolen information then using it to\nblackmail you... Damn.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	3				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	I knew it was Granin's work the minute I saw it.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	4				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	See, I'd been corresponding with him for a while.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	5				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Corresponding?\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	6				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Letters. Between scientists doing the same kind of\nresearch...\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	7				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	He always complained that nobody understood his\nideas on bipedal locomotion.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	8				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Ah... So you're the "American friend" he was talking\nabout.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	9				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Obviously he didn't write a word about the\ntechnology in his letters.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	10				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Except for one time.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	11				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	"If by chance anything should happen to me, I\nentrust my research to you.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	12				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Better that than handing it over to these ignorant\nso-called scientists."\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	13				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Sounds like him, all right.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	14				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Then one day his papers actually came.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	15				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	It wasn't hard for me to imagine what had\nhappened to him.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	16				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	I felt it was my duty to carry on his work after\nhim... And also...\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	17				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	What?\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	18				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	I thought maybe combining his technology with\nmine\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	19				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	could be a way to bring East and West together.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	20				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Like the docking of Apollo and Soyuz.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	21				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	It'll never make the history books. And it's still not\na reason to plagiarize.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	22				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	...You're right. You're absolutely right.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	23				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	I never had the courage to tell the truth, that's all.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	24				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	I always looked for excuses to cover it up.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	25				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Until now.\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	26				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	Huh...?\n
1	en	250	0xfa640	64100	27				v_bri_huy0120_010_0	28	You told me everything. You're no longer a coward.\n
1	en	250	0xfaf50	64245	0					3	"Metal Gear"...\n
1	en	250	0xfaf50	64245	1					3	Huh? What about it?\n
1	en	250	0xfaf50	64245	2					3	You mumbled that when I first explained about\nPeace Walker.\n
