1	en	255	0xffda0	65498	16					8	��J��8��3M�0PA�W���N��q��Q�\\5�D�d�emƆ�f`|"�^�lyZ���y�-�N�Bz��`�\t�P1��[�F!!�p������f�m��VV���3���T�3���j��Ң@iHfQ{�K��y=U8[XE���ve���H����:����[��a�! [��ܵu={V#��ɏ��߇hV:�����!��Vo\n�q�j��л�A����[�� =�9����N�눦|�y����c8�`�r��m��/�\n�n�A4o�m��,l%]G�
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	0				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Being an unmanned vehicle,\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	1				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	the Chrysalis can perform extreme G-force\nmaneuvers that standard aircraft can't.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	2				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	It'll be difficult to counter-attack once it's coming\nat you.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	3				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	I should mention that it's also equipped with a\nrailgun.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	4				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	A what?\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	5				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	A weapon that fires rounds using electromagnetic\nforce in lieu of gunpowder.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	6				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Wait... An electric weapon?\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	7				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Not exactly. The rounds themselves are\nconventional shells.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	8				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	It creates force by running an electric current\nthrough a magnetic field.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	9				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Fleming's left-hand rule - didn't they teach you that\nat school?\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	10				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Not at mine.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	11				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Oh. Anyway, as the railgun uses electromagnetic\nforce,\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	12				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	it needs to charge before firing.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	13				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Once you see it starting to charge, take immediate\nevasive action.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	14				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Also, as with the Pupa, destroying the AI will put\nthe unit out of commission.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	15				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Do enough damage and it'll have to land.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	16				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Use that opportunity to get in the AI pod.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	17				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Wait too long, and it'll start to repair itself.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	18				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	Be sure to shoot the hatch the moment it lands.\n
1	en	256	0x1003f0	65599	19				v_bri_mis_huy0020_000_0	20	I think I can handle that.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	0				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	During development, I had trouble figuring out how\nto handle the Cocoon's excess heat.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	1				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	I'm embarrassed to admit it, but the radiators are\nmost likely its weakest points.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	2				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	They're not heavily armored, as heat wasn't\ndissipating properly.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	3				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Where are they?\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	4				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	There are multiple radiators on the upper and\nlower sections.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	5				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Just look for the exhaust fumes.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	6				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Only thing is, the upper radiators typically aren't\nexposed.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	7				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	You should probably shoot for the lower ones first.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	8				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	You could move under its body to access the\nlower ones,\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	9				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	but the Cocoon is capable of vertical movement,\nso be careful not to get crushed.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	10				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Watch out for missiles and hedgehogs too.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	11				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Hedgehogs? Anti-submarine multiple spigot\nmortars?\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	12				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	I adapted the technology for use as a surface\nweapon.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	13				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	Both the missiles and hedgehogs take time to load,\nthough.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	14				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	It can't fire a hedgehog until the lamp comes on.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	15				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	As for the missiles, watch for the way the hatches\nmove.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	16				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	That'll be my chance to get close.\n
1	en	256	0x100aa0	65706	17				v_bri_mis_huy0030_000_0	18	When the hatches open, out come the missiles.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	0				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Number of warheads detected by the DEW Line?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	1				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Fifty-seven.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	2				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Number of MIRVs included?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	3				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Minimum twenty-nine, maximum thirty-five.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	4				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Target region?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	5				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	United States. East Coast.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	6				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Estimated time of reentry?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	7				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Two-oh-four-eight Zulu.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	8				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	President's selected attack option?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	9				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Unknown.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	10				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	The President is dead?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	11				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Unknown. Communication has been lost.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	12				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...I select Offutt Air Force Base as my target.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	13				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Offutt...? But that's a U.S. base! What on earth do\nyou mean?!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	14				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Based on the projected number of incoming\nwarheads,\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	15				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Washington D.C. is presumed destroyed.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	16				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	The President is most likely dead, the U.S.\ngovernment's control lost.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	17				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I realize that. So why not retaliate?!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	18				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	With both sides destroyed, global anarchy would\nensue.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	19				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Recovery would be... difficult.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	20				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	The United States' nuclear strike capability must\ntherefore be neutralized\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	21				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	in order to preserve the Communist bloc, where\ngovernment remains largely intact.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	22				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	You're siding with the enemy? You can't be serious!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	23				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Tell me, how do you define enemy?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	24				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	There are no borders in this world.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	25				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	The same conclusion, again and again... Where is\nthe flaw?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	26				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	All right. Let's try something else. Commencing test.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	27				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Understood.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	28				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...You're crossing a suspension bridge.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	29				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Is this my mission?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	30				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Yes. Your destination is the other side.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	31				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	The bridge is wide enough for only one person to\npass at a time.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	32				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	A man is approaching from the opposite side. He's\ncarrying a gun.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	33				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I shoot him.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	34				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Suppose he's your husband?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	35				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...I shoot him.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	36				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	In self defense?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	37				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	To spare him the grief. One must die, and one\nmust live.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	38				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Next question.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	39				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Your father asks you to fix the roof and mow the\nlawn.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	40				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	When you climb the ladder onto the roof, the\nladder is taken away.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	41				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I fix the roof.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	42				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	And when you're finished?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	43				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I jump down.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	44				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	You'll break more than bones at this height.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	45				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I see no need to change my decision. I have not\nfinished mowing the lawn.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	46				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I must remain loyal to the mission.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	47				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Next.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	48				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	There is a snake in the bushes. It is poisonous. I\nneed you to get rid of it.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	49				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I chase it back to its nest.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	50				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	You can't. The snake's too vicious. It's already\nkilled many people.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	51				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...I chase it away.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	52				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	It will bite you unless you kill it.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	53				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Go home!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	54				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Kill me... Kill me now...\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	55				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Why not kill the snake? Your mission is to get rid\nof it.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	56				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Is this what you call loyalty?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	57				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	What are you loyal to? Country? Ideology?\nFeelings?\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	58				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I... I...\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	59				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	I am loyal to myself.\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	60				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	...Who's there?!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	61				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Who's that?!\n
1	en	257	0x101110	65809	62				v_ccl_cst_0010_000_0	63	Intruder!\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	0					46	I'm about to leave for Costa Rica, a place\ncompletely unfamiliar to me.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	1					46	And on a top-secret mission for the CIA, no less.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	2					46	No guarantees I'll come back alive. Might even get\nrubbed out by the CIA itself.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	3					46	So I'm leaving behind this record, for her sake... for\nThe Boss' honor.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	4					46	Ever since I was a child, I've loved to look up at\nthe night sky.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	5					46	I'd go outside after sunset and drink in the cold air.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	6					46	The moon, Venus, so many stars floating on the\nedge of infinity...\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	7					46	In Manchester, it wasn't very often you saw the\nnight sky in all its star-studded glory.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	8					46	But it was enough to stir a deep longing inside me.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	9					46	Even as others cowered beneath Nazi air raids,\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	10					46	I was out there watching the skies, dreaming of\none day reaching the heavens.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	11					46	There were, of course, more practical concerns.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	12					46	My skin was incredibly sensitive. Even the slightest\nbit of sun would turn it an angry shade of red.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	13					46	Playing outside during the day was completely out\nof the question.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	14					46	Naturally, I hardly ever had the chance to play with\nother children my age.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	15					46	But I never felt lonely for it.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	16					46	Their way of thinking was irrational, making them\nsimple, easy to predict.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	17					46	The boys would talk of tanks and aeroplanes and\ncreepy, crawly bugs...\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	18					46	the girls of pretty dresses, glass beads and tea\nand cakes. Of boys they liked...\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	19					46	I never had much to say on such matters.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	20					46	The curious thing is, adults really aren't all that\ndifferent.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	21					46	They're simple, capricious. Especially men.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	22					46	As they get older, their heads fill with thoughts of\nwomen and more women.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	23					46	Thankfully, I always did have a head for\nmathematics.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	24					46	When I was about ten, I'd visit a Dr. Turing, who\nlived nearby.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	25					46	We'd sit and discuss mathematical logic.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	26					46	The lights were always on at his house, even in the\ndead of night.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	27					46	"Theoretically," he'd say, "there's no algorithm that\na computing machine couldn't reproduce."\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	28					46	Dr. Turing wasn't foolish like other men,\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	29					46	although I didn't find out why until later... after he\ndied.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	30					46	"The time will come," he'd say, "when computers will\nbe able to think for themselves."\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	31					46	That idea rocked me to my core.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	32					46	My aptitude for mathematics brought me closer to\nthe stars.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	33					46	I breezed through school, then went to America for\nuniversity.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	34					46	While I was studying at Caltech, NASA was\nestablished.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	35					46	I signed up in a heartbeat.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	36					46	I was a pretty good computer engineer at the time,\nand NASA needed skills like mine.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	37					46	The work was enjoyable. Even though I'd given up\non going into space myself,\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	38					46	it was a pleasure just being a part of it all.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	39					46	I was assigned to Project Mercury, America's effort\nto compete with the Soviets in manned space flight.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	40					46	Seven men were chosen as pilot candidates for\nthe program,\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	41					46	becoming heroes overnight. People called them the\n"Mercury Seven."\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	42					46	The project made good progress, more or less. We\nhad all the funds and materials we needed.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	43					46	After countless hours of analysis, we even had\nplans for something on par with Sputnik.\n
1	en	258	0x102280	66088	44					46	We thought it'd be only a matter of time before we\ncaught up with the Russians.\n
