1	en	263	0x107150	67349	0					55	The days that followed were spent training\ntirelessly for our first space flight.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	1					55	The Boss endured conditioning that tested the\nlimits of human capability,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	2					55	and I supported her. Those were happy days.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	3					55	On April 12th, the spacecraft carrying The Boss\nleft the Earth's atmosphere.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	4					55	Although it was only 20 minutes of ballistic flight,\nit was nothing short of miraculous,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	5					55	considering how little time we'd had. The first\nhuman in space. We were ecstatic.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	6					55	Then something went wrong.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	7					55	The reentry angle was slightly off, warping the\nouter hull.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	8					55	The cause was clear - it was the\nhastily-constructed window we'd installed.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	9					55	Her capsule missed the projected splashdown point\nby a long shot.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	10					55	As we raced towards the capsule, a breaking news\nbroadcast came over the radio.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	11					55	It was the news report of the successful launch of\nthe Vostock rocket,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	12					55	piloted by the cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin. They'd\nbeaten us into space by the slimmest of margins.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	13					55	When we arrived, the capsule had already\ndisappeared beneath the waves.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	14					55	The Boss was floating on the surface.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	15					55	I... can't really recall what happened next.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	16					55	They told me later that I gave a strange scream\nand plunged into the sea,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	17					55	not caring whether my skin would burn.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	18					55	She didn't wake up.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	19					55	Her entire body was bruised, burnt, scorched by\ncosmic rays.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	20					55	It was a miracle she was still alive.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	21					55	Every day, propaganda boasting of the shining\naccomplishment flowed out of the Soviet Union.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	22					55	The hardest part was having to read the report\nthe next morning from Izvestia,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	23					55	the Soviet governmental paper.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	24					55	Written there after the report were those\nlegendary words of Major Gargarin -\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	25					55	"The Earth is blue."\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	26					55	The person who should have said those words,\nthough, was incapacitated, confined to a bed.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	27					55	NASA opted to pretend the flight had never\nhappened.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	28					55	The Soviets had orbited a man around the Earth\nand returned him safely.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	29					55	We'd barely managed to escape the atmosphere\nand achieve ballistic flight.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	30					55	Even worse, the pilot was horribly mangled and\nthe spacecraft lost altogether...\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	31					55	As they saw it,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	32					55	covering it up was the only alternative to seeing\nthe nation's pride dealt yet another blow.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	33					55	I've been told\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	34					55	military records show The Boss was taking part in\nthe Bay of Pigs incident at the time.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	35					55	They were that desperate to erase the whole affair.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	36					55	In the end,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	37					55	Alan Shepard's ballistic flight one month later\nbecame known as America's first space flight.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	38					55	Shepard's flight owed its success to insights\nearned through The Boss's sacrifice.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	39					55	But to me, none of that mattered. I prayed day and\nnight for her recovery,\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	40					55	never leaving her side, not even for a moment.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	41					55	Then, as summer ended and the chill of winter\napproached, she spoke.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	42					55	"Give me water..."\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	43					55	I threw myself on her breast, and she embraced me.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	44					55	It was all I needed.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	45					55	I thought she would tell me about space.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	46					55	The true sky, where the stars don't twinkle.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	47					55	But the only thing she'd speak of was Earth.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	48					55	Our home - as she'd seen it from space. So\nfleeting, so irreplaceable.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	49					55	I was ashamed. I'd been enamored for so long with\nthe sky that\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	50					55	I'd never thought to look beneath my feet... at the\nground upon which I stood.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	51					55	As soon as her rehab was over, she was gone.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	52					55	It happened without warning. No one informed me,\nand the higher-ups wouldn't say a word.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	53					55	It wasn't so strange or surprising, really.\n
1	en	263	0x107150	67349	54					55	I simply assumed she was off to complete her next\nmission.\n
