1	en	279	0x117460	71494	0					44	The Philosophers were a secret society of\npowerbrokers formed in a pact\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	1					44	between the U.S., Russia, and China in the early\n20th century.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	2					44	Of course, by that time the American and Soviet\nbranches had already parted ways.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	3					44	But there were those among the remaining Russian\nPhilosophers\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	4					44	not entirely happy with the one-party Communist\nstate.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	5					44	The Boss reached out to them.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	6					44	She arranged clandestine meetings in Berlin, hoping\nto find a way into OKB-1,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	7					44	the Soviets' premier design bureau.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	8					44	She worked tirelessly to win their sympathy - in\nsome instances using huge sums of cash,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	9					44	in others by helping them over the Berlin Wall.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	10					44	It was a dangerous game to be playing.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	11					44	The Philosophers had everything on her. And not\njust information, either.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	12					44	She'd given birth to a child on the battlefield, only\nto have them immediately snatch it away.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	13					44	I know she told you that story.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	14					44	If that child was in the hands of the Soviet\nPhilosophers,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	15					44	she'd be putting more than just herself in danger.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	16					44	But she did what she had to do.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	17					44	At the time, the Soviet Union was believed to have\nan arsenal of missiles\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	18					44	far greater than that of the United States.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	19					44	If that proved to be the case, Moscow would be\nfree from the yoke of nuclear deterrence...\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	20					44	Raising the possibility that the Soviets might\nactually launch nukes\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	21					44	- if they felt it necessary.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	22					44	As you know, the so-called missile gap turned out\nto be a Soviet bluff.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	23					44	Moscow'd gone to incredible lengths to perpetuate\nthe lie.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	24					44	In fact, the whole space race was really just a part\nof an elaborate ruse.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	25					44	Only we didn't know that at the time.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	26					44	She used to joke that even she swallowed the\nwhole missile gap story hook, line, and sinker.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	27					44	She put her life on the line for the sake of her\ncountry - to prevent nuclear war.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	28					44	And it was because of her sacrificial efforts that\nAmerica succeeded\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	29					44	in placing a sleeper agent inside OKB-1.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	30					44	NASA began to receive huge volumes of technical\ndata from the Soviet program.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	31					44	By the end of 1959,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	32					44	they'd succeeded in sending a chimpanzee named\nSam on a ballistic rocket flight.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	33					44	The rocket never left the atmosphere,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	34					44	but all the same it was a huge success for NASA,\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	35					44	restoring confidence in its technology.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	36					44	Then, just when the operation was starting to\nproduce results, the CIA came calling.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	37					44	"You're a war hero," they said.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	38					44	"No need for you to dirty your hands with this sort\nof black ops.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	39					44	We'll take it from here."\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	40					44	In effect, they wanted to reap the rewards for\nthemselves.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	41					44	But The Boss didn't object. "My part is over," she\nsaid.\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	42					44	"I don't care what you do with the data now."\n
1	en	279	0x117460	71494	43					44	It seemed as if NASA was making great strides\ntoward manned spaceflight\n
