1	en	281	0x119010	71937	0				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The sleeper that she'd worked so hard to place\nwas a double agent.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	1				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	He'd been turned by the Soviets and was feeding\nthe Americans lies.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	2				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	But what shocked The Boss even more was why.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	3				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The CIA's access agent had been taking a large\ncut of the sleeper's pay.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	4				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The sleeper felt what little he was left with wasn't\nworth the risk.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	5				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	When he got a better offer from Moscow, he took\nit.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	6				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	At some point,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	7				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	the Americans' intelligence operation had become\nan open book to the Soviets.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	8				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	And contrary to what the sleeper'd been reporting,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	9				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	the Soviets' manned spaceflight technology was\nquite advanced.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	10				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	It wasn't until she infiltrated OKB-1 herself\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	11				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	that The Boss learned the truth about Sputnik 5.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	12				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	After the spacecraft reentered the atmosphere, it\nejected the pilot\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	13				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	- seat and all - at an altitude of 7,000 meters.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	14				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	It was the Soviets' solution to the problem of land\nrecovery.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	15				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The mystery device attached to Sputnik 5 was for\nthat purpose.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	16				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The pilot would parachute down from that high\naltitude wearing a bulky spacesuit...\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	17				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	Crazy, I know.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	18				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The Soviets knew exactly how dangerous it was, of\ncourse.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	19				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	So much so that they gave Yuri Gagarin, the first\nman in space,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	20				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	a special two-rank promotion before reentry.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	21				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	Only Moscow could come up with this kind of plan.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	22				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	With total state control over information,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	23				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	they could easily pretend it never happened in the\nevent of a failure...\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	24				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The Boss passed the info on to NASA. It was their\nworst nightmare.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	25				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The glory of mankind's first spaceflight was about\nto be stolen from them by the Soviet Union.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	26				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	It was Sputnik all over again...\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	27				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	And the CIA was caught no less off guard.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	28				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	Their ineptitude had caused the fiasco in the first\nplace.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	29				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The Soviets' deception was brilliant. They'd woven\na masterful tapestry of truth and fiction\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	30				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	to convince the Americans their intelligence\noperations were proceeding as planned.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	31				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	No one but The Boss could have seen through it...\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	32				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	It was obvious the Soviets had some gifted minds\nworking on their side.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	33				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	But that certainly didn't excuse the CIA's massive\nfailure.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	34				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The CIA wasn't done, though,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	35				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	and devised an ingenious plan to avoid taking\nresponsibility.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	36				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	Luckily for them, a new President had been elected\nthe previous November - John F. Kennedy.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	37				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	As he didn't know the case history when taking\noffice,\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	38				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	the CIA was able to feed lies to his new\nadministration.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	39				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	They masked their own failures, placing the blame\nsquarely on The Boss's shoulders.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	40				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	After all, she'd selected an unreliable sleeper.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	41				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	They claimed she'd failed to make use of one of\nthe most effective means\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	42				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	of controlling a sleeper agent's actions.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	43				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	Holding their family hostage.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	44				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The Boss was well aware that would have worked -\nshe told me so later.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	45				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	But she couldn't bring herself to do it...\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	46				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	It seems clear it had to do with having her own\nchild taken.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	47				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	She could never inflict the same pain on anyone\nelse.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	48				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The CIA used that against her.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	49				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	In his election campaign, Kennedy had vowed to\nclose the missile gap with Moscow.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	50				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	If America lost the space race, that promise would\nbe worthless.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	51				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The fact that the President had no links to the\nPhilosophers only made things worse.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	52				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	He was completely unaware of the secret networks\nshe'd used to complete her mission.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	53				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	In his eyes, she was no better than a traitor.\n
1	en	281	0x119010	71937	54				v_eva_cst_0030_000_0	55	The fate that awaited her was cruel beyond belief...\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	0					48	The order came down from above:\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	1					48	The Boss would take the official blame for the\nsleeper's betrayal.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	2					48	More so, she'd serve as the guinea pig for NASA's\nmanned spaceflight program.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	3					48	By then, NASA had gotten as far as sending a\nchimp named Ham into space.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	4					48	Now came a whole battery of ground tests to pave\nthe way for a human to take its place.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	5					48	High-G tests, zero-G tests, spacesuit tests,\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	6					48	simulations in pressurized oxygen environments...\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	7					48	Some of the tests were highly dangerous. Too\ndangerous for the Mercury Seven,\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	8					48	who were by then already media darlings.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	9					48	So The Boss was made to stand in for them.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	10					48	She could have refused the order\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	11					48	- argued her side of the story against the CIA. But\nshe didn't.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	12					48	The way she saw it,\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	13					48	her military experience made her a potentially\nvaluable asset to those tests.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	14					48	She felt it was her duty to accept.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	15					48	She soon proved herself right.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	16					48	The Boss was more than just a guinea pig.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	17					48	She drew on her experience to give the staff\nfocused advice exactly where it was needed.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	18					48	NASA's space program took off again.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	19					48	At the rate they were going, putting a man in\nspace seemed within reach...\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	20					48	The higher-ups at NASA were impressed with her\ntalents.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	21					48	They took to calling her the eighth astronaut, the\n"Mercury Lady."\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	22					48	The photo I sent you was taken around that time.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	23					48	I suppose maybe they were planning to use it as\npropaganda\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	24					48	- "the world's first female astronaut."\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	25					48	But then, just as things were looking up, they hit a\nnew snag.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	26					48	The government told them to add a window.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	27					48	There were no windows in NASA spacecraft back\nthen.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	28					48	Ham never saw the stars around him.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	29					48	But Washington insisted. Their sources told them\nSoviet spaceships had windows.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	30					48	Space exploration is a form of propaganda in itself.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	31					48	After all, what was the point of sending a man into\nspace\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	32					48	if he couldn't tell the world what he'd seen?\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	33					48	Most of all, though, they simply couldn't have the\nRussians doing something they couldn't.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	34					48	It was sheer madness, and everybody knew it.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	35					48	NASA was already facing impossible deadlines.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	36					48	Now they were adding yet another element of\nuncertainty to the mix.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	37					48	Installing a window would both weaken the\nspacecraft\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	38					48	and make it harder to shield the occupant from\ncosmic rays.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	39					48	They weren't about to put their Mercury Seven\ngolden boys in that kind of danger.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	40					48	So The Boss was chosen as the pilot instead...\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	41					48	Even if she made it up into space,\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	42					48	she was given less than a 20% chance of\nreturning to Earth alive.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	43					48	But she took it in stride,\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	44					48	even helping them make the necessary design\nchanges to the Mercury capsule.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	45					48	It was like going to work every day at NASA to\nmake her own coffin.\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	46					48	Then, without warning, the U.S. received intelligence\n
1	en	282	0x11a440	72260	47					48	that the Soviets had moved their Vostok\nspacecraft to the launch site.\n
