1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	0				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	America couldn't let the Soviets beat them with\nVostok.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	1				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	NASA was forced to cut short the testing process\nand move the launch date up.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	2				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	They pulled out all the stops to get it scheduled\nfor the earliest hours of the next day.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	3				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The spacecraft would be launched from Cape\nCanaveral in the dead of night.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	4				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	For the flight path, they chose a course that would\ntake her as far east as possible.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	5				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	She'd be able to see the Earth in daylight from the\nwindow, at least for a little while.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	6				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The Boss climbed into the Mercury capsule\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	7				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	having completed less than 80% of the regular\nflight procedures.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	8				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	Both the timing and the flight path were risky, and\ntesting was far from complete.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	9				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	But NASA had no other choice.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	10				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The Boss, for her part, never expected to come\nhome alive.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	11				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	She was going on a one-way trip. The American\nversion of Laika the dog.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	12				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	And yet she went all the same.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	13				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	"Someone has to be first," she said. "If fate has\nchosen me, then I accept.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	14				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	For my country. For the balance of the world."\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	15				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	That's how she saw things.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	16				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	"My chances may be slim, but I'll take them. I've\naccepted my fate.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	17				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	If anyone can get through this, I can."\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	18				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	Then, on April 12, at one thirty in the morning, The\nBoss entered space.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	19				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	It was nothing short of a miracle.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	20				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	First, that she'd made it in an incomplete\nspacecraft.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	21				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	And second, that her flight coincided almost\nexactly with Gagarin's.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	22				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	As the sun rose over the horizon\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	23				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	- and cosmic rays began to bombard her through\nthe window - she saw the Earth.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	24				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	I think you've heard the story before.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	25				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	It was a world without borders. A perfect jewel.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	26				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	All nations, all ideologies - they seemed so far away.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	27				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	It almost made her laugh, knowing that they'd\nrisked so much\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	28				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	to beat the Soviets by a few short minutes.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	29				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	If she'd seen Vostok from her window, she\nprobably would have waved.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	30				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	But the two spacecraft were destined for very\ndifferent fates.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	31				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	Gagarin circled the Earth and returned to the\nSoviet Union safe and sound.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	32				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The Mercury capsule, on the other hand, deviated\nfrom its planned reentry angle.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	33				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The addition of a window had thrown the capsule's\naerodynamics off balance.\n
1	en	283	0x11b5a0	72538	34				v_eva_cst_0050_000_0	35	The Boss's 'coffin' veered wildly off course, and\nbegan to plunge earthward...\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	0					52	The course deviation caused the capsule to miss\nits splashdown point by a wide margin.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	1					52	It didn't have enough time to decelerate before\ncrashing into the sea.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	2					52	The force of the impact shattered the spacecraft,\nbefore it started to sink like a stone.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	3					52	The Boss used the last of her strength to escape\nto the surface...\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	4					52	Her body was covered in bruises and scorched by\nradiation.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	5					52	It was a miracle she was still alive.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	6					52	She slipped into a coma and didn't regain\nconsciousness for six months.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	7					52	To some, what was worst of all was that\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	8					52	she had lost the space race to Gagarin by only a\nfew minutes.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	9					52	That aside, it should have been a proud day for\nAmerica\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	10					52	- its first manned spaceflight.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	11					52	Sadly, her accomplishment was kept out of the\nhistory books.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	12					52	An attempt to save face by Washington.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	13					52	In contrast to Gagarin's orbital fight, America had\nonly managed a ballistic trajectory.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	14					52	Her flight time was much shorter, too.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	15					52	And most importantly, the Soviets had brought\nback both spacecraft and pilot safely.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	16					52	NASA couldn't even conjure up a half-hearted tale\nof heroic survival.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	17					52	The coup de grace, though, was\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	18					52	the Soviet propaganda campaign that kicked into\ngear after the flight.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	19					52	"The Earth was blue..." Those were the words of\nGagarin on his return.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	20					52	And the whole world was listening.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	21					52	In the blink of an eye, Gagarin was an international\ncelebrity, earning accolades from every corner.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	22					52	It was a PR coup for the Soviet Union.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	23					52	Meanwhile, Washington could only grit its teeth in\nsilence.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	24					52	They decided there was no value in making The\nBoss' flight public,\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	25					52	that it would only make matters worse.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	26					52	So they buried it instead, labeling it a dark mission...\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	27					52	NASA and the CIA did everything in their power\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	28					52	to erase all traces of The Boss from Project\nMercury.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	29					52	The result was the photo you see before you. Not\na bad job, huh?\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	30					52	Not only did they expunge her flight from the\nrecords,\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	31					52	they denied that she ever existed.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	32					52	And that wasn't the end of it.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	33					52	As an alibi, the military claimed\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	34					52	she'd taken part in the CIA's botched Bay of Pigs\nplot to take back Cuba.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	35					52	And when The Boss was finally well enough to\nreturn to service,\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	36					52	they wouldn't take her back. She was forced to go\nunderground.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	37					52	In the public eye, The Boss was still a hero of\nWorld War II.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	38					52	In reality, though, she was anything but that.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	39					52	She was a non-person, fit only for the blackest of\nblack ops...\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	40					52	In effect, she may as well have been dead.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	41					52	Gagarin went on a worldwide tour in front of\nmillions of adoring fans.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	42					52	The Boss, meanwhile, was a walking secret,\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	43					52	unable to breath a word about her experience.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	44					52	The authorities in Washington had this to say:\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	45					52	"Why are you still here?"\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	46					52	"If only you'd had the decency to give us a nice\nquote like Gagarin\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	47					52	and then die an honorable death, you'd have gone\ndown as a hero."\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	48					52	Three weeks later, on May 5th, Alan Shepard,\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	49					52	one of the Mercury Seven, made his own ballistic\nflight.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	50					52	They sure didn't try to cover up his achievement.\n
1	en	284	0x11c2d0	72749	51					52	Of course, he owed his success to the lessons\nlearned from The Boss' tragedy.\n
